      ---------------------------------------------------------------
              Fuzzing harness for the YAML configuration loader
      ---------------------------------------------------------------


1. Theory
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The YAML configuration file is the library's main untrusted-input surface: every
byte of it flows through the backend parser and the wrapper's node lookup, type
conversion, and error reporting code before a context even exists.  The unit
tests cover the failure modes a reviewer can think of; a fuzzer covers the ones
nobody thought of by mutating inputs and keeping every mutant that reaches new
code, so the corpus gravitates toward the parser's dark corners on its own.

Coverage-guided fuzzing only works when the fuzzer can observe the code that it
exercises.  The harness therefore does not link against the regular build of
the library: fuzz-build.sh builds a separate copy of it with clang, compiled
with the -fsanitize=address,fuzzer-no-link options, so every branch taken inside
the library feeds the mutation engine and the address sanitizer checks every
heap access and reports leaked allocations on every error path.  That copy is a
snapshot of the source tree, configured and built inside the fuzzing build
directory, so a harness builds whether or not the tree itself is configured and
the tree is never written to; the snapshot is rebuilt from scratch as soon as a
library source is newer than the instrumented library.

The harness itself (fuzz-yaml.cpp) presents each input to otelc_init() through
a memfd-backed path, which keeps the file-based API intact without touching any
disk.  Each input is loaded twice: under a context name that the seed corpus
contains, so the lookup and conversion paths run deep, and under an absent name,
so that the not-found paths run as well.  A context that loads successfully
is released again through otelc_deinit(), which lets the leak checker close
the loop over the whole lifecycle.

2. Practice
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage, with the time limit in seconds and any further arguments passed on to
the libFuzzer binary unchanged:

  % ./fuzz-yaml.sh [ time-limit [ libfuzzer-args ... ] ]

The corpus persists under /tmp/otelc-fuzz/corpus and is seeded from the file
test/otel-cfg.yml plus minimal snippets; the crash artifacts are written to the
/tmp/otelc-fuzz directory.  The script requires clang, because libFuzzer and
the coverage instrumentation are clang features.

The first campaign ran about three million executions at roughly 9500 runs a
second, growing the corpus to several hundred units, and finished without any
findings at all: no crashes, no address sanitizer reports, no memory leaks,
no timeouts, and no aborts escaping the backend error-handler conversion.

3. Conclusion
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The YAML loader withstood a coverage-guided, sanitizer-instrumented attack on
top of the earlier manual review: malformed documents are rejected through the
error path, and the error path itself neither leaks nor corrupts memory.  As a
side effect the campaign proved that the library compiles warning-free under
clang with the project's -Werror posture.

The libfyaml backend was then given its own campaign, selected with the
OTELC_FUZZ_LIBFYAML variable, and it did not get far: a five-byte document, the
'%' directive marker followed by the bytes 01 a9 00 00, makes libfyaml 0.9 spin
forever inside its directive scanner, which consumed the whole budget after a
thousand executions.  A program that only builds a document from that file
reproduces the hang with no wrapper code involved, so the finding belongs to the
library and is recorded in the MEMO for an upstream report; the harness now
passes a per-input timeout so that such a hang is reported in seconds instead of
the twenty minutes libFuzzer waits by default.  Beyond that one input the
backend is still barely explored, and it deserves a longer run once the hang is
out of the way.

Longer runs, a YAML dictionary for the mutation engine, and fuzzing under the
thread sanitizer are natural extensions when more assurance is wanted.